    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-act-1991-sec-58",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "58 Section 58",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-act-1991#sec-58"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-legislation-amendment-act-1991.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-legislation-amendment-act-1991.pdf, p.20",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Section 4 of the Principal Act is amended:\n(a) by inserting \"Australian Capital Territory and the\" after\n\"includes the\" in the definition of \"State\" in subsection (1);\n(b) by inserting \"Australian Capital Territory or the\" after \"include\nthe\" in the definition of \"Territory\" in subsection (1).\n(2) Despite\nsubsection\n(1), where,\nimmediately\nbefore\nthe\ncommencing day:\n(a) an order or warrant under the Principal Act was in force; or\n(b) an application for such an order or warrant had been made but\nnot finally dealt with;\nand the indictable offence (or alleged or suspected indictable offence)\nconcerned was an offence against a law of the Australian Capital\nTerritory, the Principal Act continues to apply, on and after that day,\nin relation to:\n(c) the order, warrant or application; and\n(d) the offence, or alleged or suspected offence; and\n(e) any person convicted of the offence, or any person alleged to\nhave committed it or suspected of having committed it;\nas if subsection (1) had not been enacted.\n(3) Despite subsection (1), Part VI of the Principal Act continues\nto apply, on and after the commencing day, in relation to interstate\nrestraining orders and interstate forfeiture orders registered in the\nSupreme Court of the Australian Capital Territory before that day as\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment\nNo. 28, 1991\nif references in that Part to a Territory included the Australian Capital\nTerritory.\n(4) In this section:\n\"commencing day\" means the day on which this section commences.\nCharge on property subject to restraining order",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Section 4 of the Principal Act is amended:\n(a) by inserting \"Australian Capital Territory and the\" after\n\"includes the\" in the definition of \"State\" in subsection (1);\n(b) by inserting \"Australian Capital Territory or the\" after \"include\nthe\" in the definition of \"Territory\" in subsection (1).\n(2) Despite\nsubsection\n(1), where,\nimmediately\nbefore\nthe\ncommencing day:\n(a) an order or warrant under the Principal Act was in force; or\n(b) an application for such an order or warrant had been made but\nnot finally dealt with;\nand the indictable offence (or alleged or suspected indictable offence)\nconcerned was an offence against a law of the Australian Capital\nTerritory, the Principal Act continues to apply, on and after that day,\nin relation to:\n(c) the order, warrant or application; and\n(d) the offence, or alleged or suspected offence; and\n(e) any person convicted of the offence, or any person alleged to\nhave committed it or suspected of having committed it;\nas if subsection (1) had not been enacted.\n(3) Despite subsection (1), Part VI of the Principal Act continues\nto apply, on and after the commencing day, in relation to interstate\nrestraining orders and interstate forfeiture orders registered in the\nSupreme Court of the Australian Capital Territory before that day as\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment\nNo. 28, 1991\nif references in that Part to a Territory included the Australian Capital\nTerritory.\n(4) In this section:\n\"commencing day\" means the day on which this section commences.\nCharge on property subject to restraining order",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-act-1991-sec-58-ss-1"
        },
